The Sun, curious and a wanderer, asked the corn what it learned from the world beyond the valley. The corn spoke of patience, of roots that drink deep and leaves that drink rain, of days that stretch into nights and nights that kneel to greet the morning. The Moon listened too, and found in the grain a mirror of tides and time.

Yet the valley’s people grew fearful of such light, believing it would burn the fields or blind the eyes. So they cut the stalk and shared its kernels as if they were mere bread. But the light would not die; it slept inside the kernels, waiting for hands that would listen.

When true listening returned, the river woke again with a soft hum, guiding the valley back to growth, to balance, to the patient brightness that makes life flourish.
