When a frost-slow winter pressed in, threatening to sever seeds from the earth, Sora asked them to listen to the lantern’s roots: speak only after hearing the land’s instruction. The roots sang of patient planting, of shade for seedlings, of sharing harvests with those who had none. Following this counsel, the village opened channels for irrigation, mulched terraces, and a pledge to barter across kin and distant neighbors rather than hoard. The lantern’s light shifted toward warmth and courage, guiding hands to work in harmony with the soil.

